In .NET 10 (MAUI), `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` was deprecated/removed on Android because the underlying `adjustResize` mode was deprecated in Android 11+. The recommended approach is now **edge-to-edge rendering with `KeyboardAcceleratorsPlacement`** or handling insets directly.

**Solution: Use `android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"` replacement via the new `ImeInsets` approach:**

### Option 1 – Set `AdjustResize` in AndroidManifest (still works on most devices)

In `Platforms/Android/AndroidManifest.xml`, ensure your main activity has:

```xml
<activity
    android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
    ... />
```

### Option 2 (Recommended) – Use the .NET 10 `KeyboardInsetsBehavior`

In your `MauiProgram.cs`:

```csharp
builder.ConfigureMauiHandlers(handlers =>
{
#if ANDROID
    Microsoft.Maui.Handlers.WindowHandler.Mapper.AppendToMapping("KeyboardInsets", (handler, view) =>
    {
        var activity = handler.PlatformView as Android.App.Activity;
        if (activity?.Window != null)
        {
            // Enable edge-to-edge and let the framework handle insets
            activity.Window.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(false);
            AndroidX.Core.View.ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(
                activity.Window.DecorView, new InsetListener());
        }
    });
#endif
});
```

With a helper class in `Platforms/Android/`:

```csharp
using AndroidX.Core.View;

public class InsetListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        var systemBars = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.SystemBars());

        v.SetPadding(
            systemBars.Left,
            systemBars.Top,
            systemBars.Right,
            Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, systemBars.Bottom));

        return WindowInsetsCompat.Consumed;
    }
}
```

### Why this works

Android deprecated `adjustResize` in favor of the WindowInsets API. The listener dynamically applies bottom padding equal to the keyboard height, pushing your input bar up.

### iOS

On iOS this isn't typically an issue — MAUI already adjusts for the keyboard. If needed, wrap your content in a `ScrollView` or listen to `UIKeyboard` notifications.
